 "question": "B. Is this measure already running in City of Helsinki (or in the HUS university hospital area that treats its patients)?\nA. Does recent evidence support the size of effect we assume: does this measure reduce deaths among the people it reaches, and by how much?\nMeasure: Smoking cessation started at the first specialist visit. Every current smoker with suspected lung cancer gets cessation medication and counselling at the first visit, as part of preparing for curative treatment.",
 "area_id": "90",
 "verdict": "supported",
 "effect_check": "supports",
 "effect_summary_en": "A prospective Moscow cohort of 517 current smokers with early-stage NSCLC found that quitting after diagnosis was linked to lower all-cause mortality (adjusted HR 0.67, 95% CI 0.53–0.85).",
 "supported_en": "Annals of Internal Medicine (2021): quitting after diagnosis was linked to an adjusted HR of 0.67 (0.53–0.85) for all-cause death in early-stage NSCLC.",
 "not_supported_en": "The evidence is observational, from Russia and based on self-reported smoking. It does not show the effect of offering cessation help at the first visit, or how many patients would actually quit.",
 "in_use_here": "no_evidence",
 "in_use_scope": "none",
 "in_use_quote": null,
 "in_use_source": null,
 "in_use_summary_en": "HUS's lung cancer pathway page says that stopping smoking is key and that patients see a nurse for guidance at the first visit, and HUS offers a free online self-help programme for quitting before surgery. No page says that every smoker with suspected lung cancer is started on cessation medication and counselling at the first visit.",
 "missing_en": "",
 "summary_en": "HUS's lung cancer pathway page says that quitting smoking completely is key for smokers with lung cancer and that patients meet a nurse for guidance at the first visit. HUS also offers a free, opt-in online self-help programme for quitting before surgery, listed on Suomi.fi. No page shows that cessation medication and counselling are routinely started at the first visit, so there is no public sign the measure is running. A 2021 Moscow cohort study (Annals of Internal Medicine) found that quitting after diagnosis of early-stage NSCLC was linked to an adjusted HR of 0.67 (0.53–0.85) for death from any cause, which matches the size the model assumes.",
 "unanswered_en": "I found no HUS or City of Helsinki page showing a cessation nurse or a standing order for cessation medication for patients with suspected lung cancer.",
